    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The reset protocol: what to do right now
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If your face stings after washing, flakes, or feels tight under moisturiser, pause active treatments. An irritated barrier cannot process exfoliating acids. A five to seven day reset lets surface lipids mend, settling redness and stopping irritation bumps.
    </p>

    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Pause the acid.</strong> Stop salicylic acid for five to seven days if moisturiser stings. If your skin is dry without burning, cut back to two evenings per week.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Switch to a non-stripping cleanser.</strong> Avoid foaming washes and scrubs. Use a gentle lotion cleanser that rinses clean without leaving your face tight.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Layer humectants and ceramides.</strong> Apply glycerin or panthenol on damp skin, then seal that moisture in with a ceramide cream to rebuild the lipid barrier.<sup style="font-size:11px;line-height:0;"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605504/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[4]</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Shield with morning sunscreen.</strong> Exfoliated skin is sensitive to UV light, which worsens inflammation and darkens post-acne marks on Indian skin tones. Use broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher.</li>
    </ol>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Clinical research confirms that pairing acne routines with ceramide skincare directly improves treatment tolerance, reducing flaking and stinging while calming the background inflammation that drives reactive breakouts.<sup style="font-size:11px;line-height:0;"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39835475/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[5]</a></sup>
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How to use salicylic acid without damaging your skin
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Once your skin feels calm and no longer stings, reintroduce salicylic acid as an occasional pore-clearing tool rather than a daily scrub.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Stick to 1% to 2% salicylic acid, which clears pores effectively with minimal irritation.<sup style="font-size:11px;line-height:0;"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40465501/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[6]</a></sup> Stronger peels strip the barrier quickly without added benefit for regular home care.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Avoid stacking multiple drying products at once:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Drop physical scrubs.</strong> Rough exfoliators and cleansing brushes tear fragile surface cells and worsen breakouts.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Avoid layering strong actives.</strong> Do not combine salicylic acid with benzoyl peroxide, glycolic acid, or prescription retinoids unless advised by your doctor.<sup style="font-size:11px;line-height:0;"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39913223/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">[7]</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Treat congested areas, not single spots.</strong> Dabbing concentrated acid repeatedly onto a pimple burns surface skin. Apply a thin layer across your entire congestion-prone zone instead.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Start twice weekly.</strong> Use the acid two nights a week for two weeks. If skin stays calm, move to alternate nights. Leave-on acid rarely requires twice-daily use.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Formula design also matters. Traditional acne toners often dissolve salicylic acid in high-proof alcohol that dries the surface. Choosing a formula that builds barrier defense into the active step avoids this issue. For example, <a href="/products/acne-blemish-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Acne, Blemish Control Serum</a> pairs 1% salicylic acid with soothing Centella asiatica and a five-ceramide system with cholesterol to clear pores while supporting the barrier. In the serum's own clinical study on 36 Indian women, ages 18 to 30, with acne, lesion count was down 47.7% and comedones were down 61.6% at four weeks, with sebum down 19.4% in four weeks. Results may vary.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If skin is severely stripped, taking time out to replenish moisture with <a href="/products/deep-hydration-glow-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Deep Hydration, Glow Serum</a>, which pairs pentavitin and cica with ceramides, helps restore softness and elasticity before resuming blemish care.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Resist the urge to pick or physically scrub away peeling flakes. Buffing tears delicate new cells, triggers deeper post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation on Indian skin tones, and restarts the inflammation cycle. Let dead cells shed naturally as moisture recovers.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Matching your routine to your skin's reaction
    </h2>
    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
