    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        When to consult a dermatologist
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Over-the-counter salicylic acid works well for blackheads, whiteheads, and mild blemishes, but cannot replace medical care for deep inflammation. If you notice severe redness, facial swelling, intense itching, or hives, stop using the product immediately, as these signs indicate an allergic response.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        See a dermatologist if you have deep, painful nodules or cystic acne that fails to improve after six to eight weeks of gentle care. Scrubbing through cystic acne with stronger exfoliants increases the risk of scarring. A dermatologist can design a targeted medical routine that clears breakouts without stripping your barrier.
    </p>

    
    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Should I stop using salicylic acid if my skin starts peeling?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes, pause the acid for several days if visible peeling occurs. Peeling shows surface lipids were stripped faster than skin can regenerate them. Use a gentle cleanser and ceramide moisturiser until flaking stops, then restart twice weekly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does salicylic acid purging typically last?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">A normal purge lasts four to six weeks, matching your skin renewal cycle. Breakout intensity peaks around week three and declines steadily. New blemishes appearing after eight weeks indicate barrier irritation rather than purging.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can dehydrated skin cause more acne breakouts?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. When the barrier loses critical water, it triggers inflammation and sheds dead cells unevenly. These dry flakes mix with sebum and trap bacteria inside pores, causing fresh blemishes while the surface stays dry.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I apply moisturiser before salicylic acid to reduce dryness?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Applying a light hydrating serum or thin moisturiser before salicylic acid buffers the acid and reduces irritation. Buffering slows absorption and shields surface lipids while still allowing the acid to clear congestion over time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How often should someone in India use salicylic acid during humid weather?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">During humid Indian weather, sebum rises, but sweat and heat can also irritate an over-exfoliated barrier. Using salicylic acid two to three evenings a week is ideal. Daily application often leads to tightness and rebound oiliness.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item" style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can salicylic acid and niacinamide be used together?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes, salicylic acid and niacinamide pair exceptionally well. While salicylic acid clears pore congestion, niacinamide strengthens the moisture barrier, calms redness, and balances surface oiliness, helping prevent post-acid irritation.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    
